  "track": "track-08",
  "title": "scaling availability replication sharding load balancing",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "Scaling increases the useful work a system can handle while preserving its service goals. Vertical scaling changes the capacity of a resource; horizontal scaling adds instances or partitions, but can introduce coordination, skew, and distributed failure modes.",
    "Availability is a property of the end-to-end service path, not an instance count. Redundancy improves availability only when replicas do not share the same failure domain and failover is detected, safe, and tested.",
    "Replication copies data for availability or read capacity; partitioning divides ownership and data to distribute load. A system can replicate each shard, but replication does not itself remove a hot shard or provide a universal ordering guarantee.",
    "Load balancers distribute connections or requests using health, capacity, locality, and routing policy. The right policy depends on protocol layer, request duration, state, and failure behavior; health checks must not mark a dependency healthy merely because a process responds.",
    "Sharding makes the partition key a long-lived architectural choice: skew, hot keys, uneven growth, and rebalancing can dominate query and operational complexity. Deployment stamps create independent service slices and may accompany sharding, but are not the same concept as a database shard.",
    "Recovery design states user-visible objectives such as RTO and RPO, tests failover and failback, and distinguishes replicas from backups. Multi-region or active-active arrangements add conflict, consistency, routing, and data residency decisions."
  ],
